```

Both updates now use sales as the common entry/exit mutex, making the stock invariant shared across the two paths.

Verdict: bug found

Line 24: `printf("checksum=%d\n",checksum);`

A join only orders actions that follow its successful return. Printing first can observe unfinished data and has no ordering against the worker write.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | worker not yet finished |  | checksum initially 0 |
| 2 |  | main reads checksum | no completion edge yet |
| 3 | worker writes checksum |  | can conflict with main’s read |
| 4 |  | main joins worker | ordering came after the read |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -21,7 +21,7 @@
 static void *worker(void *p) { (void)p; checksum=21; return NULL; }
 int main(void) {
     pthread_t t; ck(pthread_create(&t,NULL,worker,NULL));
+    ck(pthread_join(t,NULL));
     printf("checksum=%d\n",checksum);
-    ck(pthread_join(t,NULL));
     return 0;
 }
```

Move join before the observation, so completion and the worker’s write happen before main reads the result.

Verdict: bug found

Line 27: `(void)p; int local_x=x;`

Only half the snapshot is protected. The outside read can also race with x’s writer, so the table is an illustrative interleaving rather than a defined raced-C outcome.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | writer not yet updating |  | x=0, y=0 |
| 2 |  | read x outside lock -> 0 | snapshot starts outside protection |
| 3 | lock(board) succeeds |  | A owns board |
| 4 | x=1 |  | y still 0 |
| 5 | y=1 |  | x=y=1 |
| 6 | unlock(board) |  | board free |
| 7 |  | lock(board) succeeds | B owns board |
| 8 |  | read y -> 1 | split snapshot can disagree |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -24,8 +24,8 @@
     ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&board)); return NULL;
 }
 static void *b(void *p) {
-    (void)p; int local_x=x;
-    ck(pthread_mutex_lock(&board)); int local_y=y; ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&board));
+    (void)p; ck(pthread_mutex_lock(&board));
+    int local_x=x,local_y=y; ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&board));
     seen_equal=(local_x==local_y); return NULL;
 }
 int main(void) {
```

Acquiring board before both reads makes the snapshot one critical section and orders all conflicting accesses.
